.TH AVR\-SERVICE\-TOKEN 1 "" "avr" "Avrea CLI"
.SH NAME
avr-service-token \- Create and manage the organization's service tokens.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B avr service-token
[OPTIONS] COMMAND [ARGS]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Create and manage the organization's service tokens.
.PP
A service token (avt_...) belongs to a group of one organization rather than to a person, and keeps working when its creator leaves. It reads only the repositories and egress firewall rules its access names. Organization admins manage service tokens while signed in (avr auth device-login or avr auth login); a scoped or service token cannot.
.PP
A service token reaches the VMs its grants name. With --max-owned-vms it creates VMs, and it administers those VMs even after VM creation is stopped. It runs avr vm create without --repo or --egress-rules, list, show, ssh, ssh-config, metrics without --watch, update with --name or --ttl, rotate-https-label, pause and delete; resume and fork are refused. avr vm capabilities needs a VM grant or --max-owned-vms. Revoking the token deletes the VMs it created.
.PP
To use a service token, set AVR_TOKEN to its secret. Its organization applies automatically; set AVR_ORG to the org-... ID in loops to save a request per command. By default, a service token has 60 requests per minute, or 31 per VM it may own when that is more, up to 600. Commands that need a person, such as avr feedback, refuse it, and avr auth logout does not revoke it: unset AVR_TOKEN instead. After a suspected leak, revoke-all revokes every service token of the organization.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
create
Create a service token and print its secret once.
.TP
list
List the organization's service tokens, soonest expiry first.
.TP
revoke
Revoke a service token.
.TP
revoke-all
Revoke every service token of the organization.
.TP
rotate
Issue a service token a new secret and print it once.
.TP
set-access
Replace a service token's grants or change its VM creation.
.TP
view
Show a service token's details, versions and grants.
